1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,089
Stir your coffee.

2
00:00:01,089 --> 00:00:03,524
The swirl grows, then it slowly dies.

3
00:00:03,524 --> 00:00:11,020
Now a strange question: could a fluid ever spin so hard that its speed becomes infinite, in a finite amount of time?

4
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:14,415
Mathematicians have asked this for almost a century.

5
00:00:14,415 --> 00:00:16,978
It is part of a million-dollar problem.

6
00:00:16,978 --> 00:00:21,655
In September 2026, OpenAI said its AI had found a way to make it happen.

7
00:00:21,655 --> 00:00:25,499
But only if you are allowed to push the fluid from outside.

8
00:00:25,499 --> 00:00:29,920
This video explains how it works, and what nobody has confirmed yet.

9
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:33,174
The official problem has four doors.

10
00:00:33,174 --> 00:00:35,164
You only need to open one.

11
00:00:35,164 --> 00:00:39,513
Doors A and B say: a fluid left alone always stays smooth.

12
00:00:39,513 --> 00:00:44,451
Doors C and D say: with some outside push, a fluid can break down.

13
00:00:44,451 --> 00:00:47,252
OpenAI claims to have opened C and D.

14
00:00:47,252 --> 00:00:49,611
Doors A and B are still closed.

15
00:00:49,611 --> 00:00:53,960
So we still do not know if a fluid left alone can do this.

16
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:56,107
Here is the first trick.

17
00:00:56,107 --> 00:01:00,813
Draw any flow you like, then ask: what push would make this flow happen?

18
00:01:00,813 --> 00:01:02,553
There is always an answer.

19
00:01:02,553 --> 00:01:05,905
You just compute what is left over in the equation.

20
00:01:05,905 --> 00:01:10,482
That sounds like cheating, and it would be, if the push went wild too.

21
00:01:10,482 --> 00:01:18,539
So the real rule is hard: the speed must blow up, but the push must stay perfectly smooth, even at the moment of the blowup.

22
00:01:18,539 --> 00:01:21,440
The fluid has to do the explosion by itself.

23
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:23,818
The flow is a tiny whirlpool.

24
00:01:23,818 --> 00:01:29,335
Water spirals toward the center, like a skater pulling in their arms and spinning faster.

25
00:01:29,335 --> 00:01:32,767
It escapes up and down the middle, so more can come in.

26
00:01:32,767 --> 00:01:35,894
Over time, the whirlpool gets thinner and thinner.

27
00:01:35,894 --> 00:01:39,020
Its width shrinks a little faster than its height.

28
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:41,962
It becomes a slim needle at one point in space.

29
00:01:41,962 --> 00:01:43,311
Here is the surprise.

30
00:01:43,311 --> 00:01:50,360
The speed goes to infinity, but the energy inside the needle goes to zero, because the needle is getting so small.

31
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:54,272
The center works fine, and so does the region far away.

32
00:01:54,272 --> 00:01:58,487
The trouble is the ring between them, where the two parts are joined.

33
00:01:58,487 --> 00:02:00,775
There, the equation does not balance.

34
00:02:00,775 --> 00:02:04,689
Something is missing, and the missing piece grows without limit.

35
00:02:04,689 --> 00:02:09,446
An outside push cannot fill that gap, because then the push would blow up too.

36
00:02:09,446 --> 00:02:11,975
So where can the missing force come from?

37
00:02:11,975 --> 00:02:13,360
From the fluid itself.

38
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:16,641
Imagine tiny waves running around that ring.

39
00:02:16,641 --> 00:02:20,297
A wave moves back and forth, so on average it goes nowhere.

40
00:02:20,297 --> 00:02:22,369
But it can still carry something.

41
00:02:22,369 --> 00:02:25,842
When fluid moves outward it carries a little extra spin.

42
00:02:25,842 --> 00:02:28,706
When it moves inward it carries a little less.

43
00:02:28,706 --> 00:02:33,885
Both motions move spin in the same direction, like a line of people passing buckets.

44
00:02:33,885 --> 00:02:37,358
Mathematicians call this kind of effect Reynolds stress.

45
00:02:37,358 --> 00:02:41,867
The construction tunes these waves so that what they carry fills the gap.

46
00:02:41,867 --> 00:02:44,792
Each wave starts from an incredibly small push.

47
00:02:44,792 --> 00:02:50,520
The fluid's own shearing makes it grow, and then viscosity, the fluid's stickiness, kills it.

48
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,532
Every fix creates new, smaller errors.

49
00:02:53,532 --> 00:02:57,468
So the construction repeats a cycle: fix, measure, fix again.

50
00:02:57,468 --> 00:03:03,562
In the paper, each round improves the error by the same fixed amount, and this goes on forever.

51
00:03:03,562 --> 00:03:08,767
What remains is a push that stays smooth all the way through the singular moment.

52
00:03:08,767 --> 00:03:16,195
And here is a beautiful detail: right at the point of the blowup, the push and all its rates of change fade to zero.

53
00:03:16,195 --> 00:03:20,892
At that place and that moment, the fluid is almost completely on its own.

54
00:03:20,892 --> 00:03:24,320
The outside push did its work earlier, and elsewhere.

55
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:27,444
We did not review the whole written proof.

56
00:03:27,444 --> 00:03:29,307
That is far beyond this video.

57
00:03:29,307 --> 00:03:34,715
But we did a simple test on the sizes in the story, using a short program anyone can run.

58
00:03:34,715 --> 00:03:38,621
Older theorems say a blowup must make certain measurements grow.

59
00:03:38,621 --> 00:03:44,149
So we checked them on the needle, and they do grow, in the way those theorems would expect.

60
00:03:44,149 --> 00:03:45,772
This is only a rough test.

61
00:03:45,772 --> 00:03:47,935
Passing it does not prove anything.

62
00:03:47,935 --> 00:03:51,000
Failing it would have been a serious warning sign.

63
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:57,250
OpenAI also released a proof written for Lean, a program that checks logic step by step.

64
00:03:57,250 --> 00:04:00,203
It is huge: over four hundred thousand lines.

65
00:04:00,203 --> 00:04:02,065
Does that settle everything?

66
00:04:02,065 --> 00:04:02,771
Not quite.

67
00:04:02,771 --> 00:04:05,082
There are three separate questions.

68
00:04:05,082 --> 00:04:08,036
One: does the code actually pass the checker?

69
00:04:08,036 --> 00:04:13,686
We ran it ourselves, on a clean setup, and two different checking programs accepted it.

70
00:04:13,686 --> 00:04:18,822
Two: does the code's final statement really say what the official problem says?

71
00:04:18,822 --> 00:04:22,738
We read it and we think so, but experts should confirm that.

72
00:04:22,738 --> 00:04:26,012
Three: is the written paper correct, line by line?

73
00:04:26,012 --> 00:04:27,746
Lean does not answer that.

74
00:04:27,746 --> 00:04:33,909
Researchers Bastounis, Circelli and Hansen reported places where the code and the paper differ.

75
00:04:33,909 --> 00:04:39,880
We confirmed one of them ourselves: the paper says four derivatives, and the code uses five.

76
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:43,026
That alone does not make the final result false.

77
00:04:43,026 --> 00:04:47,520
It does mean the code is not a line-by-line certificate of the paper.

78
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:49,724
So where do things stand?

79
00:04:49,724 --> 00:04:54,408
As far as we know, no independent peer review has confirmed this result.

80
00:04:54,408 --> 00:04:56,974
MathDB still lists the problem as open.

81
00:04:56,974 --> 00:05:03,647
News reports say experts find the proof hard to read, and that OpenAI does not plan to claim the prize.

82
00:05:03,647 --> 00:05:10,449
There is also a public dispute about credit, involving other mathematicians working on similar questions.

83
00:05:10,449 --> 00:05:13,400
Those accounts conflict, and we take no side.

84
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:15,991
Here is the idea in one breath.

85
00:05:15,991 --> 00:05:18,367
A whirlpool collapses into a needle.

86
00:05:18,367 --> 00:05:23,634
Tiny waves, powered by the fluid itself, carry force exactly where it is missing.

87
00:05:23,634 --> 00:05:26,267
Endless corrections clean up the errors.

88
00:05:26,267 --> 00:05:29,992
The speed becomes infinite while the energy stays finite.

89
00:05:29,992 --> 00:05:35,065
If this holds up, it shows what a fluid can do with a carefully designed push.

90
00:05:35,065 --> 00:05:37,506
But the older question is still open.

91
00:05:37,506 --> 00:05:41,038
Can a fluid, left completely alone, do the same thing?

92
00:05:41,038 --> 00:05:41,937
Nobody knows.

93
00:05:41,937 --> 00:05:45,726
All the mathematics here is the work of the cited authors.

94
00:05:45,726 --> 00:05:48,745
This explanation was made with the help of AI.

95
00:05:48,745 --> 00:05:50,800
Sources are in the description.
